\title{Bayesian online changepoint detection for linear models}
\description{
Bagel is a fast Bayesian online changepoint detection algorithm for linear models via posterior merging.
It can detect changes in time series in sequential mode or in batch mode.
To use it, we need to specify the feature, prior, and transformation functions and set thresholds.
The vignette introduces these functions through mean, slope, seasonal, and
autocorrelated-error examples:
\code{vignette("bagelR", package = "bagelR")}.
}
\section{Choosing a model}{

For a change in mean with known observation variance, we use
\code{\link[=bagel_kv_exact]{bagel_kv_exact()}}. For known-variance models with two or more coefficients
in the base (no-change) model, we use \code{\link[=bagel_kv_approximate]{bagel_kv_approximate()}}. When the
observation variance is unknown, we use \code{\link[=bagel_uv_approximate]{bagel_uv_approximate()}}.
}

\section{Reading the results}{

For a fitted result \code{fit}, \code{1 - weights_tau_zero(fit)} gives the posterior
probability of a change at each processed observation. We obtain the
estimated change location and the first alarm time with \code{changepoint(fit)}.
Coefficient estimates for each retained group are available through
\code{active_posteriors(fit)}, which returns posterior means and covariance
factors. Under posterior merging, each group represents one or more
candidate change locations; the no-change particle is listed first.

We use \code{weights(fit)} to inspect the recovered posterior distribution over all changepoint candidates.
The \link[=bagel-accessors]{results page}
describes these quantities in detail.
}

\section{Processing arriving observations}{

We create a detector with \code{\link[=sequential_bagel_kv_exact]{sequential_bagel_kv_exact()}} or a related
sequential constructor and pass each observation to \code{\link[=update]{update()}}. Its
return value is the current no-change weight. We record \code{1 - no_change}
and apply a stopping rule in the observation loop, as in the vignette.

We use \code{bagelR::update()}, \code{bagelR::time()}, and \code{bagelR::weights()} to
distinguish these functions from functions with the same names in other
packages.
}

